{"review":"approved","flags":[{"page":44,"note":"α₂ is printed as 2.7587 (computed from the rounded ε₂ = 0.004); with unrounded weights ε₂ = 0.0038 and α₂ = 2.7784, which the summary on p.47 uses. Round-2 weights then differ slightly (0.0193/0.1547 instead of 0.0197/0.1578); normalised values are unchanged."},{"page":40,"note":"The deck's ε uses (1/N)Σwδ with weights that sum to 1 ('version 1'); the standard AdaBoost (version 2, p.50) uses Σwδ. Both are presented; numbers in the worked example follow version 1."},{"page":13,"note":"The row labelled 'Sum' / 'Avg' shows averages (0.2, 0.5, 0.3), not sums."},{"page":27,"note":"'Effective size about 37% smaller' refers to distinct records: a bootstrap sample has N records but only ≈63.2% are distinct."}],"recall":{"defs":[["Ensemble","Combination of several base learners."],["Bagging","Bootstrap aggregating: vote over models trained on bootstrap samples."],["Random forest","Bagged decision trees with random feature subsets at each split."],["Boosting","Sequential training focusing on previous errors."],["AdaBoost","Boosting with exponential reweighting and α-weighted vote."],["Gradient boosting","Additive trees fitted to negative gradients of the loss."]],"formulas":["e_{ens}=\\sum_{i>n/2}\\binom ni\\varepsilon^i(1-\\varepsilon)^{n-i}","\\hat y=\\frac1M\\sum_mG_m(x)","\\alpha_i=\\frac12\\ln\\frac{1-\\varepsilon_i}{\\varepsilon_i}","w_j^{(i+1)}=\\frac{w_j^{(i)}}{Z_i}e^{-\\alpha_iy_jC_i(x_j)}","F_m(x)=F_{m-1}(x)+\\rho\\,h_m(x)"],"takeaways":["Ensembles need diverse members better than random.","Bagging/RF reduce variance; RF decorrelates trees by feature sampling.","AdaBoost: weighted error → α → reweight mistakes → weighted vote.","Gradient boosting fits residuals (negative gradients) with shrinkage.","XGBoost adds regularisation, missing-value handling and speed."],"confused":[["Bagging vs boosting","Parallel/variance vs sequential/bias."],["Bagging vs random forest","RF also samples features at each split."],["AdaBoost vs gradient boosting","Sample weights vs gradients."],["Version 1 vs version 2 AdaBoost","Average vs sum of misclassified weights."]],"minute":"An ensemble combines several base learners by voting or averaging; it beats its members when they are better than random and make independent errors. Bagging trains models in parallel on bootstrap samples (about 63% distinct records each) and votes, reducing variance. Random forests bag unpruned trees and sample a random subset of features at every split, decorrelating the trees and giving feature importances. Boosting trains sequentially: AdaBoost computes each member's weighted error ε, gives it a vote α = ½ ln((1 − ε)/ε), increases the weights of misclassified points and predicts by weighted vote. Gradient boosting starts from a constant and adds trees fitted to the pseudo-residuals, scaled by a learning rate; XGBoost is an optimised, regularised implementation."},"flashcards":[["No Free Lunch","No algorithm is always the most accurate",5],["Weak learner","Only slightly better than random guessing",5],["Diversity sources","Algorithms, hyperparameters, inputs, training sets",10],["Fusion rules","Sum, weighted sum, median, min, max, product",12],["Ensemble conditions","Independent members, each error < 0.5",15],["Bagging","Bootstrap samples, parallel models, vote/average",18],["Bootstrap coverage","≈ 63.2% distinct records",27],["Random forest","Bagged unpruned trees + random p features per split",30],["Boosting","Sequential; each model corrects the previous",35],["AdaBoost α","$\\frac12\\ln\\frac{1-\\varepsilon}{\\varepsilon}$",40],["AdaBoost update","$w\\cdot e^{\\pm\\alpha}/Z$ (+ if wrong)",40],["AdaBoost prediction","$\\arg\\max_y\\sum\\alpha_j\\delta(C_j(x)=y)$",45],["Gradient boosting step","$F_m=F_{m-1}+\\rho h_m$",59],["Pseudo-residual","Negative gradient of the loss",59],["Squared-loss residual","$y-F(x)$",57]],"coverage":{"topics":[{"simple":["h1"],"deep":["e-single"],"viz":[],"status":"complete"},{"simple":["h1"